A||||13||Bluebottlejunky||Learning how-to shift?||||1||
Z||000000||Bluebottlejunky||04-29-2001||10:33 PM||||Hey everyone im starting to learn how-to shift my fiero..Im pretty good at its easy..But what do you guys do when you go around corners? I usually put the car in 2nd gear? but I have been hearing more and more that u should put it in Neutral than when exiting the corner put it in gear?  What is best?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Shiner||04-29-2001||10:44 PM||||Yikes.||||reg||11
Z||000002||Formula88||04-29-2001||10:47 PM||||Typically I try to keep my car in gear, even if the clutch is pressed, and just row down through the gears as I slow down.  That way, if I need power, I just release the clutch and I'm already in the correct gear.  Race drivers always downshift into the corner and power out.  I tend to drive somewhat similarly.  Just my $.02||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||04-29-2001||10:51 PM||||It depends on the corner and the speed you are going when you are in the corner.  Neutral is not a gear I'd be in while driving a car.  Now my Fiero's rpm limiter kicks in at 60 mph in second gear, so as long as you are going less than 60 mph, second gear would be good.  <P>You were talking about a Fiero with a manual shift?||||reg||1
Z||000004||sqoach||04-29-2001||10:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR><B>You were talking about a Fiero with a manual shift?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Haha! I'd hope so!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bluebottlejunky||04-29-2001||11:19 PM||||No I have a very very rare auto..hehe ya its a stick.. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>85'GT 4sp, <BR>AOL IM: Fierofanatic2001||||reg||10
Z||000006||Mach10||04-29-2001||11:50 PM||||In my mazda, in hard corners, I'd keep the engine in the higher RPM band, above 4500rpm, and power through. In a lower-revving Fiero, I'd always keep it around 3500-4500, and power through. Don't take 'er out of gear like that, in case you need to do some fancy manouvering and so that you don't melt your syncros.||||reg||10
Z||000007||GodSend||04-29-2001||11:59 PM||||If your not too worried about the speed you come out of the corner at  (ie, you are not racing).  Then my best suggestion is to pop it into Neutral (lets assume your doing 80 km's) ... Slow er down to about 35-40 kms, and pop it into second after you have completed the turn.  This is the best way to keep the clutch holding nice for a long time <P>(managed to get the factory clutch on my Talon TSI awd turbo to last to 275,000km's , and hte only reason I changed it was because they were in there anyways and I dident want to pay more labour down the road..)||||reg||1
Z||000008||Indiana_resto_guy||04-30-2001||12:19 AM||||Pay attention to your RPM ranges while you shift up normally (not beating the pants off it!) and select the proper gear for the speed of the curve. I won't select 2nd gear unless I need to slow to 25 MPH or so, unless I'm in a hurry and want to power out. Neutral is Not a 'gear' and 'coasting in neutral' is something I would avoid in curves as you never know what lurks round the bend! (it's also illegal in most states) ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tweek||04-30-2001||12:21 AM||||Hey GodSend, a friend of mine had an early 90's eclipse turbo AWD and he needs a new tranny, any ideas on where to get one and how much they are??  thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000010||GodSend||04-30-2001||04:45 PM||||Good place to look for Canadian Talon parts is  <A HREF="http://ca.dsm.org" TARGET=_blank>ca.dsm.org</A>  ..  Its a pretty good forum, but nothing like this place  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||04-30-2001||06:46 PM||||for normal driving, shift down to gear that keeps the revs where you want it,usually while going thru the turn. Racing or performance driving using the lower gears before the corners, and bumping up the throttle to match the rpms, will slow the car and save on the brakes. Not recommended though for normal driving. I know some people run down thru the gears while stopping too. Personally, brake pads are lots cheaper than clutch disks. So a lot depends on how you want to drive at the time.||||reg||1
Z||000012||JBGehling||04-30-2001||09:08 PM||||        What's the best RPM's to shift at when racing? I just got a black 88 GT w/5-speed (fiero or otherwise). Do you run it up to red-line, or what? I can drive it perfectly around town but I just don't even know where to begin when trying to go fast  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P><BR>I know this is a stupid question, but everyone's gotta learn sometime...<P>Josh||||reg||1
Z||000013||Old Lar||04-30-2001||11:03 PM||||Max horsepower is up around 5000 rpm.  I don't like to go to red line any more in the Fiero.  The only time I was on a race track driving, I was shifting at 5000 rpm which was about 80 mph in 3rd.  Once you hit 4th, a noticable drop in performance as 4th is an overdrive as was 5th.  The track (Gateway International) was set up for road course and banked curve, straight away, back to road course. So even on the straight away I couldn't get up to max speed, because I had to slow down for the road course entry.<P>Twenty minutes of racing, I didn't break anything or hit anyting, and didn't want to push my luck. I sat back and watched others do there thing.<P>Never drive your race car to the track. If it breaks there is no way home.<P>||||reg||3
